+## Base, source, and scope
 
-## Starting point
+The base was freshly synchronized to f0e453daaf8b1af848e0bf4afd42fb730018c041, with subset's promoted score691630437 from kshitij-hash d052bc3d. This candidate retains that complete GPU algorithm, paired SHA configuration, P18 chain, Q-layout mix, native kernel arguments, producer implementation, event-wait policy and host affinity policy. The substantive change is replacement of CpuGrindSubset.h with the public r7-derived implementation in i34-9 eb9ee8f3, commit9591808166fe1ce417278f66ee4b3032f41bb9fc. The replacement is treated as one host-lane mechanism, not as a claim that each of its inherited arithmetic optimizations is independently proved faster here.
 
-The base is terrapinelf's `82d8493f` (queued at the time of writing), taken from its public submission branch:
-- its host-built epoch producers: 3 host threads with SHA-NI replace 3 small per-epoch GPU kernels, +0.93% GPU rate and −0.93% GPU energy per candidate, self-checked at start-up;
-- the warp-uniform root inverse (`QSB_ROOT_UNIFORM_WARP`);
-- the whole `de5739c9` GPU tree beneath them.
+The donor's producer-specific qhp::g_share_cpu hook is removed because the promoted floating producers do not define it and the promoted GPU host thread still spins. The CPU mask continues to exclude the current main-thread mask when the main thread was pinned. No worker is added to the spinning main core. There is no import of donor host_producers.h, blocking event waits, source defaults, compiled binaries or build stamps. Apart from that small placement adapter, the donor arithmetic and candidate enumeration are retained verbatim. The inherited header documents its Ryun1 origins and terrapinelf r7 optimizations.
 
-We change one GPU knob, `QSB_SHA_FMA_ADD` 1 → 0 (below), and replace its co-grinder (`CpuGrindSubset.h`) with ours. This is the third stage of our co-grinder work: our `889742ab` (promoted at 651.26 M/s) was the first, and our `b539d6dc` (this tree with the second stage; promoted at 665.13 M/s, GPU 630.65 + CPU 34.48 M/s) the second. This package is `b539d6dc` with `CpuGrindSubset.h` (sections 9 to 12, 14 and 15) and the host-only `tests/gpu_epochs/host_producers.h` (section 13) changed, plus the regenerated carrier header's source-hash comment. The device code and the carrier image are unchanged. It also keeps what `82d8493f`'s producers need from that header (`qsha_x4`, `qsha_iv`, `qsha_k`, `qsha_supported`).
+## Performance mechanism and evidence
 
-The co-grinder sizes itself from the process's CPU set as it was before `main()`, because the producers pin the GPU host thread to one core. Threads inherit that one-core mask, so the co-grinder's builder thread first widens its own mask to every CPU but that core; the table build and the workers inherit the wide mask. It reserves the host core and places its workers on every other logical CPU, below the producer threads (`SCHED_IDLE`). Without the widening, the table build took 10.9 s instead of 0.8 s and the unpinned workers shared two CPUs; we caught this in our gate below.
+The existing crown co-grinder contributes roughly48.31M verified candidates per second in its ranked draw. fkiene's public8009bfb9 note separates i34-9 eb9ee8f3 into634.72M GPU and59.85M CPU. That donor package scored694.57M overall and did not promote. These are published component observations, not a new local benchmark. Importing the entire donor package would also replace GPU producer scheduling and blocking waits, confounding the source of its GPU decrease relative to the crown's643.32M GPU draw.
 
-## The GPU side: `82d8493f` with `QSB_SHA_FMA_ADD=0`
+This candidate isolates the stronger CPU implementation while retaining the promoted producer and wait behavior. The hypothesis is that vector prefetch, weighted batch-affine arithmetic, scalar safegcd inversion and huge-page-aware table geometry improve CPU useful work enough to offset any coexistence cost. The mathematical headroom suggested by59.85 versus48.31M is about11.54M, but that cannot simply be added to the crown: worker placement, table construction, memory pressure and competition with producers can reduce it. The exact isolated version has not been measured and may regress. No claim of a greater-than-one-percent measured improvement is made.
 
